This independent Lean implementation was produced using Lean Constellation.
The original mathematical results and proofs remain attributed to:

D. R. Heath-Brown, The divisor function at consecutive integers,
Mathematika 31(1) (1984), 141–149. DOI: 10.1112/S0025579300010743.

Heini Halberstam and Hans-Egon Richert, Sieve Methods,
Academic Press, 1974 (reprinted by Dover in 2011).

Shenggang Xie, On the (k)-Twin Primes Problem,
Acta Mathematica Sinica 26(3) (1983), 378–384.

Apache-2.0 covers the original Lean code, generated documentation and
publication metadata. It does not relicense the cited books and papers,
third-party dependencies, or full-text development transcriptions.
The child repositories include SourceCorpus transcriptions and LC source indexes.
Local runtime records and credentials are excluded.
No endorsement by the cited authors is claimed.

The WeightedSieve quantitative almost-prime theorem is supplied at declared
availability. Consumer graph_proved is completion under this provider
contract; it does not assert that the external theorem has been proved.
